2. AWS EC2 Infrastructure Preparation
We created two EC2 instances in the same VPC.
* One instance for Kubernetes master.
* One instance for Kubernetes worker.
* Both instances communicate through their private IP addresses.
AWS Security Group configuration
Configure the following connectivity between the master and worker:
Port / Protocol	Purpose
TCP 22	SSH access
TCP 6443	Kubernetes API server
TCP 10250	Kubelet API
TCP 2379–2380	etcd communication for control-plane nodes
TCP 179	Calico BGP
IP protocol 4	Calico IP-in-IP tunnel
Allow other Kubernetes node communication ports as required by your cluster configuration.
Important AWS configuration:
* Disable EC2 source/destination checks on both instances.
* Allow IP protocol 4 between the nodes.
* Allow TCP 179 between the nodes when using Calico BGP.
* Configure reciprocal node-to-node security group rules where necessary.
(These settings allow Kubernetes nodes and Calico to communicate across EC2 instances.)
3. Linux Server Preparation
Run the following commands on both master and worker nodes.
3.1 Check operating system
cat /etc/os-release
(Displays Linux distribution and version.)
hostname
(Displays the current server hostname.)
ip addr
(Displays network interfaces and IP addresses.)
3.2 Disable swap
sudo swapoff -a
(Temporarily disables swap. Kubernetes kubelet requires swap to be disabled unless configured to support swap.)
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab
(Comments out swap entries in /etc/fstab so swap remains disabled after reboot.)
3.3 Load required kernel modules
sudo modprobe overlay
(Loads the overlay filesystem module used by container runtimes.)
sudo modprobe br_netfilter
(Enables bridge network traffic to be processed by netfilter.)
3.4 Configure kernel modules
sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf <<EOF
overlay
br_netfilter
EOF
(Creates a configuration file to load the required kernel modules automatically after reboot.)
3.5 Configure networking parameters
sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf <<EOF
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables = 1
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-ip6tables = 1
net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1
EOF
(Creates persistent kernel networking settings required for Kubernetes pod networking and IP forwarding.)
sudo sysctl --system
(Loads all configured sysctl settings.)
3.6 Verify the configuration
lsmod | grep -E 'overlay|br_netfilter'
(Checks whether the required kernel modules are loaded.)
sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward
(Checks whether IPv4 forwarding is enabled.)
4. Container Runtime – containerd
Run on both nodes.
4.1 Check containerd
sudo systemctl status containerd
(Checks whether containerd is installed and running.)
sudo systemctl enable containerd
(Enables containerd to start automatically during server boot.)
4.2 Generate containerd configuration
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
(Creates the containerd configuration directory.)
containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml
(Generates the default containerd configuration file.)
4.3 Configure systemd cgroup driver
Edit:
sudo nano /etc/containerd/config.toml
(Find the runc options and configure SystemdCgroup to true. This aligns the container runtime cgroup driver with kubelet.)
SystemdCgroup = true
4.4 Restart containerd
sudo systemctl restart containerd
(Restarts containerd to apply configuration changes.)
sudo systemctl status containerd
(Verifies that containerd is active.)
5. Kubernetes Installation
Run on both master and worker.
5.1 Install Kubernetes components
Install the Kubernetes packages from the configured Kubernetes apt repository.
The required packages are:
* kubelet – Kubernetes node agent.
* kubeadm – Cluster initialization and node joining tool.
* kubectl – Kubernetes command-line administration tool.
sudo apt-get update
(Refreshes Ubuntu package indexes.)
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet kubeadm kubectl
(Installs Kubernetes node and administration components. The package repository must be configured first.)
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl
(Prevents automatic package upgrades from changing Kubernetes versions unexpectedly.)
5.2 Verify versions
kubeadm version
(Displays the kubeadm version.)
kubectl version --client
(Displays the kubectl client version.)
kubelet --version
(Displays the kubelet version.)
6. Initialize Kubernetes Master
Run only on k8s-master.
6.1 Initialize the cluster
sudo kubeadm init \
  --apiserver-advertise-address=172.31.24.71 \
  --pod-network-cidr=192.168.0.0/16
(Initializes the Kubernetes control plane. The master private IP is used as the API server advertise address. The pod CIDR defines the IP range for Kubernetes pods.)
Important: This is the actual pod CIDR used in the later cluster configuration. Do not replace it with the earlier example CIDR 10.244.0.0/16.
6.2 Configure kubectl
mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
(Creates the kubeconfig directory for the current user.)
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
(Copies the Kubernetes administrator configuration into the user's home directory.)
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config
(Changes ownership of the kubeconfig file to the current user.)
6.3 Check master node
kubectl get nodes -o wide
(Displays the cluster nodes. The master may initially show NotReady until the CNI networking plugin is installed.)
7. Join Kubernetes Worker
Run on k8s-master to generate a join command:
sudo kubeadm token create --print-join-command
(Generates a worker join command containing a bootstrap token and CA certificate hash.)
Run the generated command on k8s-worker1. The command follows this format:
sudo kubeadm join 172.31.24.71:6443 \
  --token <TOKEN> \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:<CA_HASH>
(Joins the worker to the master using the Kubernetes API endpoint, bootstrap token and certificate authority hash. Use the actual generated values.)
7.1 Verify worker connectivity to master
nc -vz -w 5 172.31.24.71 6443
(Checks whether the worker can reach the master API server on TCP port 6443. A successful connection confirms network-level TCP connectivity.)
7.2 Check kubelet
sudo systemctl status kubelet
(Checks whether the kubelet service is running.)
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 40 --no-pager
(Displays the latest 40 kubelet log entries for troubleshooting.)
7.3 Verify cluster nodes
Run on master:
kubectl get nodes -o wide
(Verifies that master and worker have successfully joined the cluster.)
8. Calico Network Installation
We used Calico v3.33.0 with IP-in-IP networking.
8.1 Install Calico CRDs
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/v3.33.0/manifests/operator-crds.yaml
(Installs the custom resource definitions required by the Calico operator.)
8.2 Install Tigera operator
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/v3.33.0/manifests/tigera-operator.yaml
(Installs the operator responsible for managing Calico.)
8.3 Calico configuration
The Calico installation must use:
* Pod CIDR: 192.168.0.0/16
* IPIP mode: Always
* VXLAN mode: Never
* NAT outgoing: true
* Block size: /26
(These settings define the pod IP allocation and cross-node encapsulation behavior.)
8.4 Check Calico pods
kubectl get pods -n calico-system -o wide
(Displays Calico pods and identifies whether they are running on both nodes.)
kubectl get ippools.crd.projectcalico.org -o yaml
(Displays the Calico IP pool configuration, including CIDR and encapsulation settings.)
ip -d link show tunl0
(Checks the IP-in-IP tunnel interface on the Linux node.)
9. Troubleshooting Kubernetes Networking
This was one of the major issues in our project.
9.1 Check API server connectivity
curl -k --connect-timeout 5 https://172.31.24.71:6443/version
(Tests access to the Kubernetes API server from a node or host.)
9.2 Check Kubernetes Service
kubectl get svc kubernetes
(Displays the default Kubernetes API Service.)
kubectl get endpoints kubernetes
(Checks the backend API server endpoint associated with the Kubernetes Service.)
9.3 Check Calico tunnel
ip -d link show tunl0
(Displays tunnel interface status and packet information.)
9.4 Check worker networking
kubectl get pods -o wide
(Displays pod IPs and node placement.)
9.5 Check Kubernetes API from a test pod
kubectl exec net-test -- curl -k -v --connect-timeout 5 https://10.96.0.1:443/version
(Tests whether a pod can reach the Kubernetes API through its ClusterIP Service.)
9.6 Check DNS from master
kubectl exec dns-master-test -- nslookup kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local 10.96.0.10
(Tests DNS resolution through the CoreDNS Service from the master-side test pod.)
kubectl exec dns-master-test -- nslookup kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local 192.168.235.196
(Tests DNS resolution directly through a CoreDNS pod IP.)
9.7 Check DNS from worker
kubectl exec dns-worker-test -- nslookup kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local 192.168.235.196
(Tests direct communication from the worker-side test pod to a CoreDNS pod on the master.)
kubectl exec dns-worker-test -- nslookup kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local 10.96.0.10
(Tests DNS through the CoreDNS Service from the worker.)
9.8 Networking fix applied in AWS
We identified that the Calico IP-in-IP packets were not reaching the other node.
Fix: Added IP protocol 4 to the AWS Security Group rules for node-to-node communication.
(Protocol 4 is IP-in-IP encapsulation. It is not TCP port 4 or UDP port 4.)
After applying this change, the worker DNS tests succeeded and resolved:
kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local
10.96.0.1
10. Argo CD Installation
Run on master.
10.1 Create namespace
kubectl create namespace argocd
(Creates the dedicated namespace for Argo CD.)
10.2 Install Argo CD
kubectl apply -n argocd -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml
(Installs Argo CD components in the argocd namespace. Use a pinned release manifest for repeatable production deployments.)
10.3 Verify installation
kubectl get pods -n argocd -o wide
(Checks Argo CD pods and their status.)
kubectl get svc -n argocd
(Displays Argo CD Services, including argocd-server.)
11. Access Argo CD Dashboard
11.1 Port forwarding
kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443
(Forwards local port 8080 to the Argo CD HTTPS service port 443.)
Open the dashboard:
https://localhost:8080
(Use this URL on the machine running the port-forward command. For remote access from your Mac, use an SSH tunnel rather than exposing port 8080 publicly.)
11.2 Retrieve initial password
kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret \
  -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d; echo
(Retrieves and decodes the initial Argo CD admin password, if the initial secret still exists.)
Username:
admin
